    "response": "In short, **the all-male composition of the panel is unlikely to be evidence of discrimination against you**, but it may highlight departmental demographics or a procedural irregularity under university policy.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what that composition likely means, along with factors to consider regarding the outcome:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Gender Discrimination Is Unlikely\nBecause both you and the successful candidate are men, an all-male panel does not point to bias based on sex in this hiring decision. Legally and practically, it is difficult to construct a claim that an all-male committee discriminated in favor of or against a candidate on the basis of sex when the candidate hired belongs to the same gender category.\n\n*(Note: If you suspect bias, age is sometimes a factor in academia when a department opts for a candidate at the dissertation stage over an experienced academic in their forties. However, proving age discrimination is difficult, especially if the department can point to legitimate criteria like industry experience or curriculum needs.)*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Departmental Demographics in Economics\nSenior academic faculty in economics departments—particularly at public institutions—continue to skew heavily male. Committees are often populated by tenured or senior faculty, heads of department, or specific subject leads. An all-male committee is frequently a byproduct of:\n* A department with few or no senior female faculty.\n* Departmental efforts to avoid overburdening the few women faculty with excessive \"service work\" or committee appointments.\n\nWhile modern best practices discourage all-male panels, their existence often reflects structural demographics rather than an intent to influence the outcome.\n\n---\n\n### 3. University Policy and Procedural Irregularities\nMany public universities have explicit human resources or Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) policies requiring diverse search committees (e.g., requiring at least one woman or an external equity observer). \n\n* **Is it a sign of something?** It could indicate that the department bypassed standard HR guidelines or received a waiver due to staffing constraints.\n* **Should you make something of it?** Filing a grievance or raising this formally is unlikely to overturn the hiring decision or result in an offer, and it carries the risk of damaging your professional reputation in a relatively small academic field.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Why the Other Candidate May Have Been Chosen\nAcademic hiring decisions at the final stage rarely come down to raw publication counts or years of teaching alone:\n* **Strategic Fit:** If the department is launching or expanding professional, executive, or applied courses, someone with \"strong experience from industry\" may fill an accreditation or curriculum gap that a purely academic CV does not.\n* **Specialization / Subfield:** The specific research field (e.g., econometrics, macro, health economics) often matters more than the raw paper count.\n* **Interview & Job Talk:** Performance during the job talk, perceived teaching philosophy, and perceived \"collegiality\" play a substantial role once a candidate reaches the final shortlist.\n\n---\n\n### 5. The Delay in Written Feedback\nTwo weeks without a reply is standard for academic hiring. Search committees typically cannot send written feedback until:\n1. The formal contract is signed and finalized with the selected candidate.\n2. The university's central HR or Dean’s office signs off on the search report and any written feedback.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n* **Do not raise the panel's gender makeup.** It will not provide leverage and will likely come across as adversarial without supporting a claim of sex discrimination.\n* **Follow up patiently:** Give it another week to ten days before sending a brief, polite follow-up email. Frame your request purely around professional development:\n  > *\"I understand the committee has a heavy schedule, but I would remain very grateful for any brief feedback or guidance that might help me strengthen future applications.\"*\n",
